This paper proposes a novel approach to semantic segmentation of very fine resolution urban scene images using a bilateral awareness network that combines the strengths of transformers and convolutional neural networks (CNNs). The proposed method, called Transformer Meets Convolution (TMC), leverages the ability of transformers to capture long-range dependencies and the spatial hierarchical representation capabilities of CNNs.

The TMC model consists of three main components: a transformer encoder, a convolutional encoder, and a bilateral fusion module. The transformer encoder processes the input image as a sequence of patches, allowing it to capture global contextual information at multiple scales. The convolutional encoder is used to extract local features from the image, which are then combined with the transformer output through the bilateral fusion module. The fusion module utilizes a novel attention mechanism that enforces bidirectional communication between the transformer and convolutional feature maps, enabling the model to adaptively weight the importance of different features based on their relevance to each other.

Evaluated on two publicly available datasets, Cityscapes and Mapillary Vistas, TMC demonstrated improved performance compared to state-of-the-art methods, achieving an overall mIoU of 86.2% and 87.5%, respectively. Additionally, extensive ablation studies were conducted to analyze the effectiveness of the individual components and provide insights into the working mechanisms of TMC.

The key contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows:

* Proposing a novel architecture that seamlessly integrates transformers and CNNs for semantic segmentation tasks;
* Introducing a bilateral fusion module that enables bidirectional communication between transformer and convolutional feature maps;
* Demonstrating superior performance of TMC over existing state-of-the-art methods on two challenging urban scene datasets;
* Providing comprehensive ablation studies to dissect the effects of various components and shed light on the working mechanisms of TMC.

This work has significant implications for applications such as autonomous

This paper proposes a novel approach for polarimetric synthetic aperture radar (SAR) image semantic segmentation, which combines the advantages of 3D discrete wavelet transform (DWT) and Markov random field (MRF). The proposed method exploits the spatial and spectral information in polarimetric SAR images by using 3D DWT to decompose the data into different frequency bands. Then, MRF is employed to model the dependencies between adjacent pixels in each band, enabling the integration of both local and global contextual knowledge. The proposed framework is evaluated on several datasets collected from different platforms and scenarios, demonstrating its superiority over existing methods in terms of accuracy and computational efficiency. Specifically, our method achieves an average overall accuracy of 90.5% and 86.7% for two publicly available datasets, outperforming other state-of-the-art techniques. Moreover, we show that incorporating 3D DWT and MRF components improves the baseline method by 10.4% and 20%, respectively. Additionally, experimental results demonstrate that our method exhibits robustness against various environmental conditions such as weather, illumination variations, and sensor noise.

The main contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows:

* A novel Polarimetric SAR image semantic segmentation method combining 3D Discrete Wavelet Transform and Markov Random Fields;
* Exploration of the complementary information provided by polarimetric SAR data through the use of 3D DWT;
* Integration of local and global contextual knowledge utilizing MRFs;
* Demonstration of improved performance compared to state-of-the-art methods on multiple datasets under varying environmental conditions;
* Quantitative analysis highlighting the effectiveness of the individual components of the proposed framework.

This research has significant implications for applications such as military surveillance, disaster response, environmental monitoring, and agricultural management, where accurate semantic understanding of radar imagery is essential. Our findings contribute towards bridging the gap between raw polarimetric SAR data and high-level interpretations, ultimately facil

In this paper, we present a novel domain adaptation framework for medical image segmentation, which addresses the problem of segmenting images from unseen domains. Our approach leverages a combination of domain-invariant feature learning and adversarial training to improve the generalization ability of deep neural networks to new domains. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method on several benchmark datasets, including lung tumor segmentation, brain structure segmentation, and retinal vessel segmentation. Our experiments show that our proposed method outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods in terms of segmentation accuracy, especially when dealing with large domain shifts. Additionally, we perform an ablation study to analyze the contribution of each component of our framework, providing insights into the working mechanism of our approach. The main contributions of this work can be summarized as follows:

* A novel domain adaptation framework for medical image segmentation that combines domain-invariant feature learning and adversarial training;
* Experimental validation of the proposed method on several publicly available datasets, demonstrating improved performance compared to state-of-the-art methods, especially when facing large domain shifts;
* An ablation study to investigate the effectiveness of different components of the proposed framework, providing valuable insights into its working mechanism.

Overall, our work has important implications for improving the robustness of deep learning models for medical image segmentation tasks, where domain shift is a common challenge due to variations in imaging protocols, patients, or diseases.

> High-level computer vision tasks are essential in modern industries like self-driving, video surveillance and augmented reality (AR). Among them, visual tracking that can robustly detect target objects across multiple frames has attracted tremendous attention [1 – 3]. Since most trackers rely on manually designed features or handcrafted models with limited representational capacity, many efforts have been made to design end-to-end neural architecture search methods, such as SANet[4] in order to obtain effective and differentiable feature modules automatically via reinforcement learning approach and achieve significant improvements over its counterparts. Despite progress with existing methods, these approaches still lack diversity of solutions since they only consider cell selection instead of other building blocks. For example, the channel number remains fixed at 256 throughout all layers in SANet due to the limitation of computation cost during RL training. Moreover, current search space fails to contain convolution kernel size variation without losing generality because each layer is searched independently rather than treated as a whole.
> In this work we propose an autonomous design pipeline called CHASE that searches optimal network structures along three dimensions including channel number, group ratio, kernel size for both spatial cells and channels at module level. Experiments show our proposed design is more compatible with backpropagation based optimization process compared to conventional ones. Compared to state-of-the-art solution of 98% accuracy, we successfully achieve above performance by adopting less computing resources using VGG-M model. Our contributions are summarized as follows:
> • We enable the search methodology to handle kernel size variations under certain constraints;
> • An efficient backpropagation method is introduced for differentiating modules’ output which requires no prior knowledge about the corresponding gradient;
> • A unified differentiable framework is provided for efficiently converging through extensive experiments.

We study sum-of-squares (SoS) relaxations for the problem of graph coloring from the point of view of exactness. Given a graph $G = (V,E)$, the $k$-coloring problem is to find a map $f : V \rightarrow \{1,2,...,k\}$ such that no two adjacent vertices are mapped to the same color. We consider the problem of finding the largest $k$ such that $G$ admits a $k$-coloring, which is equivalent to finding the chromatic number $\chi(G)$ of $G$.

The SoS hierarchy is a natural hierarchy of relaxations for $\chi(G)$, where the $k$-th level of the hierarchy is given by the following optimization problem:

\begin{equation*}
\max \sum_{i=1}^{k} x_i^2 \ \text{s.t.} \ \sum_{i=1}^{k} x_i \leq 1, \ x_i \geq 0 \ \forall i \in \{1,2,...,k\}, \ x_i x_j = 0 \ \forall \{i,j\} \in E.
\end{equation*}

The problem is to find the largest $k$ such that the $k$-th level of the hierarchy is exact, i.e. admits a solution that is also a solution to the original problem. In this paper, we show that the $k$-th level of the hierarchy is exact for all graphs of chromatic number at most $4$. Furthermore, we show that the $k$-th level of the hierarchy is not exact for any graph of chromatic number greater than $4$.

## Plot synopsis[edit]

The book begins with an attack on a military base by terrorists who disguise themselves as American soldiers. One terrorist in particular, Youssef Shamoon (nicknamed "Lion-Eye"), manages to use his knowledge and skills garnered while serving as part of Saddam Hussein's elite Republican Guard to elude capture, even killing or wounding 10 law enforcement officers during the breakout. At another location in Washington DC, two FBI agents enter into the basement of the Justice Department building where terrorist files are stored and accidentally release 65 boxes filled with evidence proving that both George W Bush and Dick Cheney were responsible for allowing the attacks on September 11, 2001 against New York City occur while they intentionally stood by doing nothing during the tragedy. A secret court, which was investigating the alleged complicity between then President George W. Bush and ex-Vice President Richard B. Cheney concerning their role in the attacks, has been abruptly halted after President Obama orders the Federal Marshall assigned to carry out all court directives be killed before he can deliver over those very same documents to Congressional leaders at a hearing scheduled later on that day.^{[8]} ^{[9]}^{[17]}
Bush finds himself under attack personally as his personal chef, Jovan Krayewski, delivers him some cake, but uses this opportunity to feed him drugs that induce a paranoid schizophrenic episode (a reference to Dan Quayle). At one point during the paranoia episode, former president Jimmy Carter is brought into a meeting room where they find Bush sitting naked around a glass table covered in drugs. Unaware that Bush had taken these drugs forcibly and unaware of them, he is told by Carter that it was really only Vice President Biden that gave out drugs so freely during Barack Obama’s presidency and the Democrats aren’t drug dealers despite Republican propaganda.^{[13]}^{[34]}^{[41]}
Cheney subsequently fancies himself a kind of political deus ex machina, taking charge due to Obama being declared dead shortly afterward by having a piece of paper stuck underneath a belt buckle with the phrase "God Bless America - Raise High

Oh, my people! How we have fallen!
How far removed our minds seem to be in this season;
the enemy has gotten to us and now we are no longer strong:
we fight amongst ourselves when really, together it could all change, but not only just for you and I but also generations upon generation behind us will gain something special;
for those of which we have lost sight of their dreams and potentials; they were simply trying to live in great unity and love as well because being one’s self isn't always an easy choice. It can feel like walking through hot coals without shoes on or brushing your teeth against cement walls instead of using toothpaste -- either way - it hurts too much!! So let me tell YOU what I would do if given any chance at all: first thing outta bed morning make sure there isn't anything left undone around house so everything gets cleaned up properly - then check emails etc until lunchtime hits whereon go pickup snacks before heading off into work…

To thine own self be true,
And it must follow, as the night the day,
Thou canst not then be false to any man.

Farewell, my dear, dear friend, and brother;
Farewell, the truest, tenderest lover;
Farewell, my own dear mother.
